WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.217
Welcome to Module 2 of the
HR-Driven Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.217 --> 00:00:06.938
run contractors from their own source.

3
00:00:06.938 --> 00:00:12.026
This is Gate 2, Sources, with
Chapter 02 of the developer workbook.

4
00:00:12.026 --> 00:00:15.281
In Module 1 you named one
writer for every account state,

5
00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:19.812
and saw that inbound provisioning writes
the leave date and the active value.

6
00:00:19.812 --> 00:00:24.212
This module applies that to the
people HR doesn't hold: contractors.

7
00:00:24.212 --> 00:00:28.328
You'll compute each contractor's
earliest end from several conditions,

8
00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:31.676
send the result through the
same middleware as employees,

9
00:00:31.676 --> 00:00:36.210
reconcile what the contractor register,
the card system and the directory say,

10
00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:37.815
without changing anything,

11
00:00:37.815 --> 00:00:41.512
and write a sponsor review that
ends access when nobody answers.

12
00:00:41.512 --> 00:00:44.988
The code runs on fictional
contractors, on your laptop.

13
00:00:44.988 --> 00:00:50.650
It's illustrative: unit-tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

14
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.888
Why does this gate exist?

15
00:00:55.888 --> 00:00:57.763
Employees come from HR.

16
00:00:57.763 --> 00:00:59.225
Your contractors don't.

17
00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:03.351
They live on C O R spreadsheets,
on a suitability register,

18
00:01:03.351 --> 00:01:05.863
or in the PIV sponsorship records.

19
00:01:05.863 --> 00:01:10.762
And when a contract is cut short, nobody
updates the end date in a spreadsheet.

20
00:01:10.762 --> 00:01:14.413
That end date is what keeps
the contractor's access alive.

21
00:01:14.413 --> 00:01:18.737
So in this module you give contractors
an end date that can't be left open.

22
00:01:18.737 --> 00:01:22.717
You compute each contractor's
earliest end from the contract,

23
00:01:22.717 --> 00:01:26.462
the completion date, suitability,
the card and the sponsor.

24
00:01:26.462 --> 00:01:29.362
You send it through the same
middleware as employees.

25
00:01:29.362 --> 00:01:33.657
And you reconcile what the register,
the card system and the directory say,

26
00:01:33.657 --> 00:01:36.410
without changing anything in any of them.

27
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.971
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

28
00:01:42.971 --> 00:01:45.335
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

29
00:01:45.335 --> 00:01:49.881
You'll name the end conditions that
make up a contractor's earliest end,

30
00:01:49.881 --> 00:01:51.997
and say why the earliest one wins.

31
00:01:51.997 --> 00:01:54.791
You'll run the extract,
read its decisions,

32
00:01:54.791 --> 00:01:57.585
and hand the extract to the HR middleware.

33
00:01:57.585 --> 00:02:02.848
You'll run the read-only reconciliation
and explain each C R finding it reports.

34
00:02:02.848 --> 00:02:05.399
And you'll describe the
sponsor review settings

35
00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:07.823
that end access when a
sponsor doesn't respond.

36
00:02:07.823 --> 00:02:11.000
You need Module 1 first,
for one fact in particular:

37
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.884
inbound provisioning is the writer of
the leave date and of the active value.

38
00:02:15.884 --> 00:02:18.126
Lab 1 uses the path's usual setup,

39
00:02:18.126 --> 00:02:22.523
and also calls the H C M to Entra
middleware from the same clone.

40
00:02:22.523 --> 00:02:27.710
Lab 2 needs a worldwide or G C
C test tenant, never production.

41
00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:34.723
Start with the guide's Step
2, and notice its order.

42
00:02:34.723 --> 00:02:37.404
First, measure before changing anything;

43
00:02:37.404 --> 00:02:40.797
the reconciliation you'll
run in the lab is read-only.

44
00:02:40.797 --> 00:02:44.197
Second, settle the six questions
with the register's owner.

45
00:02:44.197 --> 00:02:47.635
Third, ship the contractor
leaver before the joiner.

46
00:02:47.635 --> 00:02:49.048
Why the leaver first?

47
00:02:49.048 --> 00:02:53.304
Because the leaver closes the gap:
a spreadsheet end date that nobody

48
00:02:53.304 --> 00:02:58.010
updates keeps access alive, and the
middleware's earliest-end rule closes it.

49
00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:00.010
Then read where the design comes from.

50
00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:05.316
Chapter 06 of the Federal Modernization
Workbook's HR-Driven Identity

51
00:03:05.316 --> 00:03:10.172
book explains why contractors fall
outside Federal HR 2.0's scope.

52
00:03:10.172 --> 00:03:14.373
Its Appendix A gives the contractor
end conditions and their order.

53
00:03:14.373 --> 00:03:18.774
And the contractor evaluation recommends
an agency-owned register now,

54
00:03:18.774 --> 00:03:22.590
with the Oracle contingent
worker as a later source swap.

55
00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.240
Here's the heart of the
gate: the earliest end.

56
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.665
A contractor can stop needing
access for several reasons,

57
00:03:32.665 --> 00:03:34.378
and the rules file lists them.

58
00:03:34.378 --> 00:03:35.853
The contract ends.

59
00:03:35.853 --> 00:03:39.002
The completion date comes
earlier than the contract end.

60
00:03:39.002 --> 00:03:40.965
Suitability is withdrawn.

61
00:03:40.965 --> 00:03:44.165
The card is terminated,
revoked or expired.

62
00:03:44.165 --> 00:03:49.140
The sponsor separates, plus a grace
period, thirty days in the program's file.

63
00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.090
Or the sponsor misses a recertification,
every hundred eighty days in the file.

64
00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:57.102
The extract computes every
condition that applies

65
00:03:57.102 --> 00:03:59.553
to each contractor and
takes the first one.

66
00:03:59.553 --> 00:04:03.066
That's the earliest end, and
it becomes the leave date.

67
00:04:03.066 --> 00:04:07.370
A missing contract end isn't treated
as no end; the record doesn't go

68
00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:11.915
through at all, because a contractor
is never provisioned open-ended.

69
00:04:11.915 --> 00:04:17.570
The grace and recertification periods are
design guidance: your agency sets them.

70
00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:22.632
Two sets of rules.

71
00:04:22.632 --> 00:04:27.021
The extract's source checks, C
S, look at each register record.

72
00:04:27.021 --> 00:04:31.945
No contract end is C S oh one,
and the record is quarantined.

73
00:04:31.945 --> 00:04:38.307
A sponsor who isn't in the HR export is
C S oh two: no manager, so no account.

74
00:04:38.307 --> 00:04:41.906
A sponsor who has separated
is a C S oh three warning:

75
00:04:41.906 --> 00:04:47.046
access ends after the grace period
unless someone records a new sponsor.

76
00:04:47.046 --> 00:04:49.469
Then the reconciliation's rules, C R,

77
00:04:49.469 --> 00:04:52.895
compare the register, the
cards and the directory.

78
00:04:52.895 --> 00:04:56.632
C R oh one is an enabled
account past its earliest end.

79
00:04:56.632 --> 00:05:01.895
C R oh two is an orphan, an enabled
contractor account with no register record.

80
00:05:01.895 --> 00:05:05.433
C R oh three is an active
card the source doesn't hold.

81
00:05:05.433 --> 00:05:08.183
C R oh four is a record with no account.

82
00:05:08.183 --> 00:05:11.819
And C R oh five is a leave date
that isn't the earliest end,

83
00:05:11.819 --> 00:05:15.160
so the leaver workflow
fires on the wrong day.

84
00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:20.060
Two more pieces.

85
00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:22.810
First, the extract doesn't send anything.

86
00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:26.924
It writes a file in the HR middleware's
layout, and the middleware from

87
00:05:26.924 --> 00:05:31.760
Chapter 01 of Building the Attribute-Driven
Migration builds the skim requests.

88
00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:36.760
Its dry run is its default, so nothing
reaches a tenant unless you ask it to.

89
00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:40.966
And in Module 1's register, inbound
provisioning already names both

90
00:05:40.966 --> 00:05:45.322
the HR app and the contractor-source
app, through that same middleware.

91
00:05:45.322 --> 00:05:47.284
Second, the sponsor review.

92
00:05:47.284 --> 00:05:50.910
It goes on the contractor access
package's assignment policy.

93
00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:52.360
It runs twice a year.

94
00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:57.248
The manager, who is the sponsor,
reviews, with a named fallback reviewer.

95
00:05:57.248 --> 00:06:00.511
And when nobody responds,
access is removed.

96
00:06:00.511 --> 00:06:05.086
One caution from the kit: using the
requestor's manager as a reviewer

97
00:06:05.086 --> 00:06:09.540
is among the things it lists as
unconfirmed, so Lab 2 checks it.

98
00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:14.015
Try it.

99
00:06:14.015 --> 00:06:18.877
Lab 1 runs in the hr-lifecycle
folder, on fictional contractors.

100
00:06:18.877 --> 00:06:23.096
Step one lists the reconciliation's
only read: one GET on users,

101
00:06:23.096 --> 00:06:28.803
with two read permissions, the lifecycle
information read and User dot Read dot All.

102
00:06:28.803 --> 00:06:31.577
Step two computes the earliest ends.

103
00:06:31.577 --> 00:06:36.575
You give the extract the contractor
register, the card records, the sponsors

104
00:06:36.575 --> 00:06:41.177
from the HR export, the rules file, and
an as-of date, the first of November.

105
00:06:41.177 --> 00:06:42.966
It finds nine contractors.

106
00:06:42.966 --> 00:06:44.165
Three are active.

107
00:06:44.165 --> 00:06:47.652
Four are inactive, already
past their earliest end.

108
00:06:47.652 --> 00:06:51.166
And two are quarantined, so they
won't be provisioned at all.

109
00:06:51.166 --> 00:06:53.682
The source checks report two findings,

110
00:06:53.682 --> 00:06:57.615
one warning and one information
item, and the exit code is two.

111
00:06:57.615 --> 00:07:02.340
The last line tells you the next step:
build the skim requests with the middleware.

112
00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.120
The output is wrapped and trimmed to fit.

113
00:07:08.870 --> 00:07:11.744
Step three prints the nine decisions:

114
00:07:11.744 --> 00:07:15.545
each contractor's action,
earliest end and reason.

115
00:07:15.545 --> 00:07:17.470
Look at the four inactive ones.

116
00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:22.507
Each is inactive for a different reason:
a completion date, suitability withdrawn,

117
00:07:22.507 --> 00:07:27.470
a sponsor gone past the grace period, and
a sponsor recertification that came due.

118
00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:30.053
The last one is the knowledge-check case:

119
00:07:30.053 --> 00:07:33.733
whatever the contract says,
the earliest condition wins.

120
00:07:33.733 --> 00:07:38.491
The two quarantined records are nine
hundred thousand and seven, which has no

121
00:07:38.491 --> 00:07:41.540
contract end, and nine
hundred thousand and eight,

122
00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:43.696
whose sponsor isn't in the HR export.

123
00:07:43.696 --> 00:07:46.570
Step four hands the
extract to the middleware.

124
00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:49.474
It reports seven records
ready, in one batch,

125
00:07:49.474 --> 00:07:53.508
and four of them are disables,
the four inactive contractors.

126
00:07:53.508 --> 00:07:58.800
Nothing is held, and nothing is sent:
the middleware only builds the requests.

127
00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:04.213
Step five reconciles.

128
00:08:04.213 --> 00:08:08.932
You give it the same register, cards,
sponsors and rules, plus an export of the

129
00:08:08.932 --> 00:08:14.262
contractor accounts in the directory, and
it reports seven findings and one warning.

130
00:08:14.262 --> 00:08:18.887
Four are C R oh one: accounts still
enabled past their earliest end.

131
00:08:18.887 --> 00:08:23.399
Those four are exactly the four disables
the middleware built in step four,

132
00:08:23.399 --> 00:08:27.475
so once those disables are sent,
the next reconciliation shows none.

133
00:08:27.475 --> 00:08:29.400
There's one orphan, C R oh two,

134
00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:33.425
an enabled contractor account
with no register record.

135
00:08:33.425 --> 00:08:37.900
One C R oh three, an active card
for someone the source doesn't hold.

136
00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:41.560
One C R oh five, a leave date
that isn't the earliest end,

137
00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:45.050
so the leaver workflow
would fire on the wrong day.

138
00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:49.638
And one warning, C R oh four, a
register record with no account.

139
00:08:49.638 --> 00:08:53.580
The reconciliation changes
nothing; it only reports.

140
00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:00.955
Step six writes the sponsor
review settings, for review.

141
00:09:00.955 --> 00:09:03.567
The tool says so: nothing was sent.

142
00:09:03.567 --> 00:09:06.105
Print the file and read four values.

143
00:09:06.105 --> 00:09:08.937
The expiration behavior is remove access,

144
00:09:08.937 --> 00:09:13.955
so a missed recertification ends access,
just as the earliest-end rule says.

145
00:09:13.955 --> 00:09:17.142
The primary reviewer is
the requestor's manager,

146
00:09:17.142 --> 00:09:20.567
at level one, with a single
named user as the fallback.

147
00:09:20.567 --> 00:09:24.651
The recurrence is absolute
monthly with an interval of six,

148
00:09:24.651 --> 00:09:27.068
so the review runs every six months.

149
00:09:27.068 --> 00:09:29.718
And reviewers have
fourteen days to answer.

150
00:09:29.718 --> 00:09:31.506
The file also names its target:

151
00:09:31.506 --> 00:09:35.726
merge these settings into the
contractor package's assignment policy,

152
00:09:35.726 --> 00:09:40.018
and send the whole policy with the
Graph PowerShell cmdlet after review.

153
00:09:40.018 --> 00:09:43.517
Step seven runs the tests,
and fifty-seven pass.

154
00:09:43.517 --> 00:09:45.354
Then delete the output folder.

155
00:09:45.354 --> 00:09:48.720
The output on the slide
is trimmed and reflowed.

156
00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:58.733
Lab 2 runs the gate in your own test tenant,
worldwide or G C C, never production.

157
00:09:58.733 --> 00:10:01.057
Set up a contractor provisioning app.

158
00:10:01.057 --> 00:10:04.676
Send two test contractors through
the middleware's apply path,

159
00:10:04.676 --> 00:10:06.695
then reconcile against the tenant.

160
00:10:06.695 --> 00:10:10.162
And apply the review settings
to a test contractor package;

161
00:10:10.162 --> 00:10:14.195
the chapter names the cmdlet and
permission for updating the policy.

162
00:10:14.195 --> 00:10:18.202
The middleware's identity holds
one permission, the synchronization

163
00:10:18.202 --> 00:10:22.070
upload permission, scoped to the
contractor provisioning app only.

164
00:10:22.070 --> 00:10:27.008
The reader app holds User dot Read dot
All and the lifecycle information read.

165
00:10:27.008 --> 00:10:31.836
To back it out, restore the policy's previous
review settings from your read-back,

166
00:10:31.836 --> 00:10:36.593
pause the provisioning job, which keeps
its state, delete the test contractors,

167
00:10:36.593 --> 00:10:40.544
remove the permissions, and delete
the export and output folders.

168
00:10:40.544 --> 00:10:43.050
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

169
00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:48.187
The federal requirement.

170
00:10:48.187 --> 00:10:53.494
This gate supports NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three A C two,

171
00:10:53.494 --> 00:10:57.738
account management, and P S seven,
external personnel security.

172
00:10:57.738 --> 00:11:00.436
It supports I A four, enhancement four,

173
00:11:00.436 --> 00:11:04.701
which identifies each person
by status, such as contractor.

174
00:11:04.701 --> 00:11:11.212
And it supports O M B M-22-09's expectation
that enterprise identity draws on HR,

175
00:11:11.212 --> 00:11:14.512
contract management
and personnel security.

176
00:11:14.512 --> 00:11:18.687
Your organization still sets the
organization-defined parameters,

177
00:11:18.687 --> 00:11:21.837
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

178
00:11:21.837 --> 00:11:28.150
The evidence an ISSO keeps for Gate 2 starts
with both reconciliation output folders

179
00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:32.863
and their manifests: one run before
the contractor leaver ships,

180
00:11:32.863 --> 00:11:35.175
and one after, with zero C R oh one.

181
00:11:35.175 --> 00:11:38.487
Then the extract and the
middleware's provenance manifest.

182
00:11:38.487 --> 00:11:42.212
The authority-per-fact table,
signed by the governance body.

183
00:11:42.212 --> 00:11:46.860
And the privacy office's determination
for the register and the card system.

184
00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:52.585
Check yourself with two questions.

185
00:11:52.585 --> 00:11:55.841
First: a contractor's
contract runs to January,

186
00:11:55.841 --> 00:11:59.697
but their sponsor recertification
was due in October.

187
00:11:59.697 --> 00:12:01.522
Which end does the extract use?

188
00:12:01.522 --> 00:12:06.973
The answer is b: the earliest condition
that applies, the recertification due date.

189
00:12:06.973 --> 00:12:10.873
The extract computes every end
condition and takes the first.

190
00:12:10.873 --> 00:12:14.347
Second: what does a C
R oh one finding mean?

191
00:12:14.347 --> 00:12:18.323
The answer is a: an enabled
account past its earliest end.

192
00:12:18.323 --> 00:12:21.959
An account with no register
record is the orphan,

193
00:12:21.959 --> 00:12:25.597
C R oh two, and a record with
no account is C R oh four.

194
00:12:25.597 --> 00:12:30.710
Once the disables are sent, the next
reconciliation shows no C R oh one.

195
00:12:30.710 --> 00:12:35.980
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

196
00:12:39.730 --> 00:12:40.555
To sum up.

197
00:12:40.555 --> 00:12:43.705
You computed nine
contractors' earliest ends.

198
00:12:43.705 --> 00:12:48.355
You built the skim requests that disable
four of them, without sending anything.

199
00:12:48.355 --> 00:12:53.158
You reconciled the register, the cards
and the directory, and found the four

200
00:12:53.158 --> 00:12:55.482
accounts those disables will close,

201
00:12:55.482 --> 00:12:58.580
plus an orphan, a stray card
and a wrong leave date.

202
00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:02.843
And you wrote the sponsor review that
removes access when nobody responds.

203
00:13:02.843 --> 00:13:06.668
Next is Module 3: make
joiners ready on day one.

204
00:13:06.668 --> 00:13:10.943
Its workflow files include the contractor
leaver workflow from this gate.

205
00:13:10.943 --> 00:13:14.573
You'll keep Lifecycle Workflows
definitions as reviewed files,

206
00:13:14.573 --> 00:13:19.095
lint them before they reach the tenant,
plan each change against what the tenant

207
00:13:19.095 --> 00:13:23.670
holds, and measure how many new hires were
actually ready on their first morning.
